{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: canada.ca \u00b7 CRA due dates and payment dates (individuals); important dates for RRSPs; line 20800; CCB; CRA T4127 (2026) \u00b7 Examples, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Dana's tax year started on January first. Her first paycheque: two thousand, five hundred and fifty-two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It ended on December thirty-first. Her last paycheque: two thousand, eight hundred and six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But the smartest tax move of her whole year didn't happen in that year at all."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It happened in February of the next year. And it was worth about one thousand, four hundred and eighty-two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A move a lot of people don't realize they're allowed to make."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Today we follow one Canadian tax year, month by month, from the first paycheque to the tax return. And you'll see exactly where the money moves."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "All numbers: the twenty twenty-six tax year, Ontario, a ninety thousand dollar salary, paid every two weeks. Examples, not real people. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "First: what a tax year is."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For individuals in Canada, the tax year is the calendar year. January first to December thirty-first."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Everything you earn in those twelve months goes on one return. And you file that return in the spring of the next year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So every tax year really runs about sixteen months. Twelve months of earning, and then a few months of settling up."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, January."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "On January first, C P P and E I start over. The yearly limits reset."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's why Dana's January paycheques are her smallest of the year. Two thousand, five hundred and fifty-two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "January is also a good time to check your T D one form. That's where you claim your personal tax credits with your employer."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "New job, second job, or a big change in your life? Update it. Remember Lena, with two jobs and a five thousand dollar bill. That started with a T D one."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And if you know you'll owe, the same form lets you ask for extra tax on every paycheque."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, the spring. But not this year's taxes."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In the first months of the year, you're still finishing last year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "R R S P contributions made in the first sixty days of the year can count for the year before. For the twenty twenty-five tax year, that deadline was March second, twenty twenty-six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Then, April thirtieth. That's the deadline for most people to file last year's return, and to pay any balance owing."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If you or your spouse are self-employed, you have until June fifteenth to file. But a balance owing is still due on April thirtieth."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, the summer."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In July, the Canada Child Benefit is recalculated. It's based on your family net income from the year before."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's why filing on time matters even if you owe nothing. Your return is what your benefits are built on."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And if you pay tax by instalments, June fifteenth and September fifteenth are two of the four due dates. The others are March fifteenth and December fifteenth."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, the fall. This is the part most people notice without understanding it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Dana's paycheques start to grow. By paycheque twenty, she reaches the yearly E I maximum. Then regular C P P stops, and a few paycheques later, C P P two."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Her last paycheque of the year: two thousand, eight hundred and six dollars. Two hundred and fifty-four dollars more than in January."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It isn't a raise. She simply finished paying the year's C P P and E I. On January first, it all starts again."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "With two jobs, each employer takes C P P and E I separately. If the total goes over the yearly maximum, the extra comes back on your return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, December."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "December thirty-first closes the tax year. Your income for the year is now fixed."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Most tax moves for the year have to happen by then. The R R S P is the big exception."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Dana, her employer withheld about seventeen thousand and thirty-four dollars of income tax over the year. And that's about what her real tax comes to."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So if she did nothing else, her return would land close to zero. No refund, no bill."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "But Dana does one more thing."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In February twenty twenty-seven, she contributes five thousand dollars to her R R S P. Inside the first sixty days, so it counts for twenty twenty-six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Her payroll never saw it. Her employer withheld tax as if her income were ninety thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But on her twenty twenty-six return, that five thousand dollars comes off her income."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So the tax she already paid is now more than her real tax. The difference comes back as a refund. About one thousand, four hundred and eighty-two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's the move from the start of this video. Made in one year, for the year before."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And remember from our R R S P video: you don't have to deduct it right away. If next year's income will be higher, you can save the deduction for later."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, the return itself."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Early in the year, your employer gives you a T four slip. It shows your income, and the tax, C P P and E I that were taken off."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Your return adds up every slip, every deduction and every credit. It compares the tax you really owe with the tax already withheld."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "More withheld than you owe? A refund. Less? A balance owing."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A refund doesn't mean you paid less tax. It means you paid too much during the year, and you're getting your own money back."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "After the C R A processes your return, you get a notice of assessment."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Keep it. It shows your R R S P deduction limit for the next year, among other things."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If the C R A changed anything on your return, the notice shows that too."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And then the cycle starts again. A new January. New limits. A new first paycheque."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's your tax year checklist."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. January: check your T D one, especially if you have more than one job."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. Before the R R S P deadline: decide if a contribution makes sense for last year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. By April thirtieth: file, and pay anything you owe."
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. July: check your benefits. They depend on the return you filed."
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Five. After you file: keep your notice of assessment."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, what did we learn?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L60",
   "file": "vo/L60.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. The tax year is the calendar year, but settling it takes until spring."
  },
  {
   "id": "L61",
   "file": "vo/L61.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. Your paycheque grows in the fall, then resets in January."
  },
  {
   "id": "L62",
   "file": "vo/L62.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Some of the best moves for a tax year happen after it ends."
  },
  {
   "id": "L63",
   "file": "vo/L63.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. Your return compares what you paid with what you owe. That's your refund or your bill."
  },
  {
   "id": "L64",
   "file": "vo/L64.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: what happens to your taxes when you work remotely. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
